Language: en

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:02.090 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  [Music]  what do you do when your web service  loses access to the internet well if the  service is running on Google Cloud you  file a support case like this one from a  real Google Cloud customer they've  rebooted the virtual machine they've  tested the network but something's very  wrong the case gets filed with a  priority of P1 production down  that means Google Cloud's support  response will follow the sun no matter  what time of day or night the ticket  will route to a regional support team  that's online in this case it's time for  a little trip to Zurich Switzerland we  usually have people who like to solve  problems  and uh in some cases we we are lucky  enough to get very interesting problem  this is Antonio at the time of this  story he was a Technical Solutions  engineer at Google Cloud support team in  Zurich his job solved the case of the  missing Network packets by the time  Antonio receives the case the customer  has found a workaround so the issue is  no longer critical but the concern the  mysterious network problems could still  pop up on other machines so we set up a  meeting with the current agent and one  of the engineers from the from the  company we started trying to  understanding a bit better the issue  depending on some issues that they are  usually common to many customers when  you go and we check like these two three  things that are always well 90 90 of the  case they are the  um  in this case however the reason why the  case was not solved yet was because all  The Usual Suspects were no at fault  because Google Cloud works hard to  ensure customer privacy Antonio doesn't  have access to the virtual machine  himself so every idea about fixing the  problem has to be passed along to the  customer to test on their side One By  One The Usual Suspects are eliminated by  the end of Antonio's shift there's still  no explanation for why the network  packets aren't making it onto the  customer's machine the case follows the  sun around to the next support team  while Antonio gets some rest  by this time the case is famous inside  Google Cloud support another support  engineer uses python to create a raw DNS  packet and sends a request to the  virtual machine's metadata server but  when the customer runs the code the DNS  reply comes back successfully as strange  as it sounds this confirms that the  networking problem is actually not a  network problem it's something wrong  with the software on the VM itself  Google Cloud support could say you know  this is your configuration this is not  some thinking we support so please fix  your configuration but at this point I  was kinda invested it was a bit like  reading a tree there and then getting to  the point where you know that the  investigator found out who is the who's  the Killer and you don't realize it yet  by now Antonio is back on shift and has  decided to take personal ownership of  the problem from now on the good news is  that the customer has offered to give  him an image of the virtual machine so  he can run tests safely without so much  back and forth so we had a lot of  conflicting information this is when we  took a step back and we tried to analyze  in a more scientific way let's say there  are only so many places where these DNS  packets could disappear so like any good  detective Antonio begins to form  hypotheses and rule them out one by one  the operating system libraries use the  call DNS are working fine DNS packets  and configuration files on the machine  look correct reinstalling the kernel  doesn't fix anything  there's nothing wrong with the firewall  configuration and DNS requests and  replies are correctly being sent and DNS  resolution fails even when bypassing the  metadata service that leaves one  possibility a bug in the Linux kernel  itself so I spent some more time maybe  one day troubleshooting the issue inside  the Linux scan and I managed to find  where exactly in the Linux kernel the  packet was dropped and why and at that  point I just created a pull request and  fix the bug on the Kernel  main release an integer overflow deep  within the Linux kernel turns out to be  the source of the trouble the bug is  patched the case is closed the customer  can move on and Google Cloud's support  detectives await their next mystery to  solve  join us next time for another case from  Google Cloud support files  [Music]

